
{"id":"1895aa6a-8f6b-4083-aa88-05ab255089c5","object":"chat.completion","created":1785035207,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if node.get('symbol') in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        symbols = set()\\n        for other in nodes:\\n            if other['id'] in retained and other['id'] != nid:\\n                symbols.add(other['symbol'])\\n        if all(req in symbols for req in required_symbols):\\n            retained.remove(nid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removal_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` according to the spec. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" from Perses-like context. The input payload has `nodes` array (tree structure with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and `required_symbols` list.\n\nObjective: given a tree with root and nodes, with required symbols that must be preserved. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain. The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes in order of decreasing depth? Or breadth-first? \"Before shallower nodes\" means deeper nodes first. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove root, any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, or a node that has children that are retained (i.e., if we decide to retain a child, we cannot remove its parent). Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We must ensure that after removal, all required symbols still appear somewhere in the retained tree. \n\nWe need to produce `removed_node_ids` (order of actual removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted). Also need to handle tie-breaking: use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth.\n\nWe must implement a deterministic algorithm. The payload includes node objects with id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable, utility. Only nodes with `removable: true` can be removed. But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" implies we can only remove removable nodes? Probably not all nodes are removable: the input has `removable` field. So we only consider nodes where `removable == true` for removal.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means we must check if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, if so cannot remove. Also if any child node is retained, we cannot remove the node. So when processing, we must consider the current retention status.\n\nWe should start with all nodes retained initially. Then process deeper nodes first (max depth descending). For each node that is removable and not root and not required symbol, we check if by removing that node (i.e., moving its children up? Actually Perses can delete nodes or replace? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But in this simplified version, it seems we only delete nodes? The output has `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`. No mention of replacement. So likely we are just deciding to delete some nodes (and their subtrees?) Or maybe removal of a node removes that node and its descendants? But careful: In Perses, deleting a node removes that node and all its children become children of the parent? Or it replaces a node with its children? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the output only has removed and retained nodes, not replacements. Also the requirement \"never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that if we remove a node, we must also remove all its children? Or we must ensure that we don't remove a node if any of its children are retained (i.e., the removal would orphan them?). In a tree, if you remove a node, its children become direct children of the parent? That is typical in Perses: deletion of a node replaces it with its children (or removes it and bridges children?). But here they say \"a node with retained children\" meaning we cannot remove a node if it still has children that are retained. So when we remove a node, we must also remove its children (or we must remove children first? Or we cannot remove a node if any descendant is retained). The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests we process leaves first, and can remove leaf nodes. Then possibly remove internal nodes after children are removed.\n\nThus the algorithm: we want to remove as many nodes as possible (or only some? There's utility field but not used clearly. The objective is \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But the interestingness oracle is \"required_symbols\" - we must preserve that each required symbol is present in the retained tree. Also the root is always retained. Possibly we want to minimize retained nodes? Or just produce some reduction? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to decide which nodes to remove. The tie-breaking: depth first, then node ID.\n\nGiven the fixtures, they likely expect a specific output. We need to infer the exact algorithm. Let's analyze the first case payload:\n\nnodes:\nroot (depth0, nonremovable)\nn1 (depth1, parent root, symbol s1, nonremovable)\nn2 (depth1, parent root, symbol s2, removable)\nn3 (depth1, parent root, symbol s0, nonremovable)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, symbol s1, removable)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, symbol s2, nonremovable)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, symbol s0, removable)\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nWe need to decide removal order.\n\nLet's try to simulate. We process deeper nodes first: depth 2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n5 nonremovable, skip. n4 removable? yes. Is n4's symbol s1 required? Required symbols include s1, so cannot remove node with required symbol. So n4 cannot be removed. n6 removable, symbol s0 not required. Check if n6 has retained children? It's a leaf (depth 2, no children listed) so no children. Check if all required symbols remain if we remove n6: required symbols are root and s1. root is root, s1 is present in n1 and n4. Both will remain. So tentatively remove n6. So remove n6 first. Now n6 removed.\n\nThen process next depth? Continue with same depth? After removing n6, we should re-evaluate? The algorithm might be: iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, and attempt removal. But since we removed a node, it may affect parent consideration? But parents are shallower, not yet processed. So we can just go through sorted list once, because we will later process shallower nodes that may have had children removed.\n\nAfter n6 removal, next node in depth 2 order: n4? already considered not removable because required. n5 nonremovable. So depth 2 done.\n\nNow depth 1 nodes: n1 (nonremovable), n2 (removable), n3 (nonremovable). Process n2: symbol s2, not required. But n2 has child n4 which is retained (n4 not removed). So \"never remove a node with retained children\" -> n2 has retained child n4, so cannot remove. n1 nonremovable, n3 nonremovable. So no more removals.\n\nThus removed: n6. retained: all others? Actually n6 removed, so retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? The root is always retained. output should have removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids: sorted list.\n\nBut wait, is that the expected result? Let's check the second case to see if pattern matches.\n\nSecond case payload similar but values differ. But algorithm should be deterministic. Let's test second case: nodes same structure, identical required symbols. n6 removable, symbol s0. n4 removable but required symbol s1? required includes s1, so n4 cannot remove. n2 removable but has child n4 (retained). So again only n6 removable. So same result.\n\nThird case: same structure, only n6 removable? Also n2, n4 removable but n4 required, n2 has retained child. So only n6 removable.\n\nThus for these three cases, the result would be identical. But the spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output for each case. The output schema requires arrays. So we can compute.\n\nBut we must be sure about the algorithm: Is it that we only remove nodes that are leaves (no children) and not required and removable? And after removing, do we need to consider that the parent might become eligible? For internal node removal, we cannot remove if it has retained children. So we only remove leaf nodes that are removable and not required and whose removal doesn't violate required symbols. That matches.\n\nBut what about the order of removal among leaves? Deeper nodes first, then by node ID tie break. So we need to sort all nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending. Then iterate, attempt removal if conditions hold (removable, not required symbol, no retained children, and after removal all required symbols still present). Since we iterate in that order, leaf nodes will be considered first. After removing a leaf, its parent might lose a child, but we consider parent later if it appears in the list (since parent has shallower depth). When we later consider parent, we check if it has retained children. If all its children are removed, then it has no retained children, and could be removed if removable and not required. That matches the concept of \"deeper nodes first\" allowing removal of internal nodes after leaves are gone. But in the fixtures, none of the internal nodes become eligible because n2 has n4 which is required and cannot be removed. n1 has n6 which is removed, but n1 is nonremovable. So not applicable.\n\nBut we must handle general case. So the algorithm should:\n\n1. Build a mapping from node id to node dict, and also child relationships: children list for each parent.\n2. Initialize set of retained node ids = all node ids.\n3. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison? ID is alphanumeric, e.g., \"n1\", \"root\". Use standard string comparison.)\n4. For each node in that sorted order:\n   - Check: node id is in retained? (if already removed, skip)\n   - Check: node is not root? (root id is \"root\" usually)\n   - Check: node's removable == True\n   - Check: node's symbol not in required_symbols\n   - Check: node has no children that are retained (i.e., for each child id in children[node.id], child id is not in retained)\n   - Check: if we remove this node, is there still at least one node with each required symbol in retained? We can compute: For each required symbol, does there exist a retained node (excluding this node) with that symbol? Since we haven't removed yet, we can check by iterating over all retained nodes (excluding this node) and ensure each required symbol appears. But careful: if multiple nodes have the same symbol, removal still okay if another node provides it. So we need to check for each required symbol, there is some retained node (other than current) with that symbol. This is a global check. But we could also precompute counts. For efficiency we could compute per symbol count, but with small size fine.\n   - If all conditions satisfied, then remove this node: remove from retained set, and record node id in removal_order list.\n5. After processing all nodes, remaining retained set is retained_node_ids (sorted).\n6. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of retained ids}.\n\nBut note: \"removed_node_ids\" is the order of actual removal, so we should preserve the order we removed them (the iteration order of successful removals). That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable, so safe. If required_symbols includes \"root\", root is retained by default.\n\nWe must also handle if a node is required but removable=false? required symbol nodes might be nonremovable in the data. But we still check to be safe.\n\nAlso, what if after removal, there is still a removed node that had required symbol? That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to implement `solve(case)` in Python using only standard library. Input `case` is the payload dict (with \"nodes\", \"required_symbols\", possibly \"_case_nonce\" ignored). We should not mutate case.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Extract nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dict id->node, and children dict: for each node, id as key, node dict value. Also build parent mapping? But we need children: iterate nodes, for each node, if parent not None, add child to parent's children list.\n- Initialize retained set with all node ids.\n- Create list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (using string compare). We can get node objects for sorting.\n- Removal order list.\n- For each node in sorted list:\n    if node['id'] not in retained: continue\n    if node['id'] == 'root': continue? Actually root is \"root\" as seen in fixtures. But better to check parent is None? The root has parent null. So check if node['parent'] is None -> root, skip.\n    if not node.get('removable', False): continue\n    if node.get('symbol') in required_symbols: continue\n    # Check children: get children list from children dict, for each child id, if child id in retained, then has retained children, skip.\n    children_ids = children.get(node['id'], [])\n    has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_ids)\n    if has_retained_child: continue\n    # Check required symbols still present after removal\n    # Build a set of symbols present in retained nodes excluding this node\n    symbols_after = set()\n    for other_node in nodes: # could iterate over retained set but need node's symbol\n        if other_node['id'] in retained and other_node['id'] != node['id']:\n            symbols_after.add(other_node['symbol'])\n    # Also root likely has symbol 'root' so included.\n    all_required_ok = all(req in symbols_after for req in required_symbols)\n    if not all_required_ok: continue\n    # Proceed to remove\n    retained.remove(node['id'])\n    removal_order.append(node['id'])\n- After loop, retained_sorted = sorted(retained) (since node ids are strings)\n- Return dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nOne nuance: The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" is already checked. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We check that.\n\nPotential issue: the global check for required symbols might be expensive but fine for small trees.\n\nAlso note: the \"utility\" field is present but not used in the algorithm from spec. The spec mentions utility in nodes but the objective doesn't mention maximizing utility. So we ignore utility.\n\nWe must ensure deterministic: sorting by depth descending then id ascending. Use node['depth'] as integer, node['id'] as string.\n\nNow, output exactly as per EXACT OUTPUT CONTRACT: object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nAlso we must wrap implementation in a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final answer must be a JSON object containing a single key \"implementation\" whose value is a Python source string.\n\nWe need to escape that string properly.\n\nI'll write the Python code in a string, then create a JSON object with that string. We must ensure it's valid JSON: escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. I'll write the code as a standard Python function.\n\nLet's write:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\n    \n    retained = set(nid for nid in node_by_id)\n    removal_order = []\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # root check: parent is None\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\n            continue\n        if node.get('symbol') in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check children\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # check required symbols\n        # build set of symbols in retained nodes except current\n        symbols = set()\n        for other in nodes:\n            if other['id'] in retained and other['id'] != nid:\n                symbols.add(other['symbol'])\n        if all(req in symbols for req in required_symbols):\n            # remove\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removal_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nWe must also add error handling? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should validate that the input has required fields, nodes list, etc. But the cases are valid, but we can add checks: if required_symbols empty? root is always there? Possibly raise if required_symbols not present? But spec says \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" We can trust the input is valid. But to be safe, we can add a check that required_symbols is a list and nodes list exists, else raise ValueError. We'll include that.\n\nAlso, ensure we don't mutate case. We are only reading.\n\nNow, we must produce the JSON output as specified: entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a Python source string properly escaped.\n\nI'll write the code as a multiline string, then use json.dumps to escape it. But I need to output manually. Better to construct the JSON manually to avoid escaping issues. I'll write the Python source as a single line with \\n escapes inside the JSON string. However, it's easier to write the Python source normally and then encode it as a JSON string. Since I'm the AI, I'll manually escape newlines and quotes.\n\nI'll produce the implementation string with proper indentation. To minimize escape, I'll write it as a triple-quoted string? But need to be within JSON string. So I'll just write the code with \\n and \\t etc. I'll ensure backslashes are escaped (e.g., \\\\n).\n\nI'll produce the final JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n  